[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> reports and says they are one bug with
one fix queued. They are not. The desk merged them by subject line
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and a subject says how a report feels,
not what broke. Read by tenant, channel and body, #4471 holds three problems. The queued parser
fix closes only the first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. The parser fix will not close the booking-v3 hint reports or the one-day moves.
Each problem now has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

### `parser` — day-first dates read month first

This one covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports. The shared parser reads every date
month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so any date with a day of 12 or less
comes out flipped: 7 April becomes 4 July. It happens in the app, by email, and on the
booking-v2 form. This is the bug the queued fix is for.

### `hint` — booking-v3 tells people to type month first

This one covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports, all through the booking form of the three
tenants on booking-v3. The form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the
hint says, and the form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The parser is not in
the path, so the parser fix leaves these as they are. The fix is the hint, or the form's reading
of it.

### `shift` — the day moves by one, the month stays

This one covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="shift" /> reports, and they describe no flip at all. The meeting moves
by one day: "Tuesday 12 May" in the invite, Monday 11 May in the calendar; a client in Lisbon
and a client in Toronto see different days for one meeting; an invite arrives before its booking.
This points at time zones or DST, not at day and month order. The desk had already read these
as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) but left them counted under `parser`.

## How to read the old table

The table on #4471 counts the same reports by cause, with the one-day moves still under
`parser`:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What was wrong with the count

- **Merged on subject.** Ten of the twelve subject lines in #4471 are shared by reports of two
  or three problems. "Date is wrong" covers all three.
- **Intake tags are a guess.** `intakeTag` follows the reporter's browser. Reporters on an
  `en-US` browser were tagged `hint` wherever they booked. Use the tenant and the channel
  (`causes.ts`), not the tag.
- **The export overlaps.** The ticket system's pages repeat some reports. Count each id once.

## For the desk

- Before a ticket is marked known, check that one fix closes every report in it.
- When a report does not describe the ticket's bug, give it its own ticket, not a footnote.

